<!--
    angr test_tail_tail_bytes_ret_dup :: tail_bytes
    (binaries/tests/x86_64/decompiler/tail.o, x86-64, ELF relocatable object).

    In an ET_REL `.o` a libc no-return like `__stack_chk_fail` is an UNDEFINED
    EXTERNAL symbol (UND, NOTYPE, size 0, no address of its own).  kuna's
    analysis-tier known-no-return pass (`noreturn_known`, default on) keys its
    facts on the ADDRESS of a *defined* FUNC symbol, so it never marks the UND
    extern.  At decompile flow time the call to `__stack_chk_fail` resolves to a
    display NAME (the relocation supplies it) but the symbol's no-return flag is
    false, so `FlowEnvironment::query_call_no_return` returns false, no
    artificialHalt(noreturn) is planted, and flow runs straight off the end of the
    function into the NEXT function.  tail.o::tail_bytes (615 bytes,
    0x401e80..0x4020e7) renders as 326 lines / 9 gotos, swallowing several
    neighbour functions, vs angr's 96 lines / 1 goto.

    Stage model: S2 "flow-classification" sub-stage.  The P0 assertion `option
    noreturn_extern on|off` toggles a name-based no-return fallback at the flow
    seam: when the address-keyed flag is unset, a CALL whose resolved callee name
    matches the known ELF no-return list (`__stack_chk_fail`, `abort`, `exit`, ...,
    leading `_` stripped, `std`-only namespace guard) is treated as no-return.
    Default OFF (only undefined-extern no-returns are affected, i.e. `.o` targets).

    Minimal reconstruction of the construct: `victim` at 0x1000 has a normal `ret`
    on the non-fail path and, on the fail path, a `call __stack_chk_fail` (the
    UND extern at 0x1040) as its LAST act with NO `ret` after it.  The bytes after
    that call are the NEXT function `next_func` at 0x100a, whose body is a
    `call neighbor_marker` (0x1050).  With the gate OFF the fall-through past the
    no-return call decodes `next_func`'s body INTO `victim`, so `neighbor_marker(`
    leaks into victim's decompilation; with the gate ON flow stops at the
    `__stack_chk_fail()` call and `victim` is bounded correctly.

    Pass 1 (option noreturn_extern off): assertion #1 proves the bug - the
    neighbour function's `neighbor_marker(...)` call leaks into victim.
    Pass 2 (option noreturn_extern on): assertion #2 proves the fix - the
    neighbour call is gone (`neighbor_marker` count drops to 0 across both passes,
    so min=1/max=1 holds only because of pass 1).  Assertion #3 proves
    `__stack_chk_fail()` is still emitted as the bounding call in BOTH passes.
-->
<bytechunk space="ram" offset="0x1000">
85ff7501c3e836000000e841000000c3909090909090909090909090909090909090909090909090909090909090909090909090909090909090909090909090c3909090909090909090909090909090c3
</bytechunk>
<symbol space="ram" offset="0x1000" name="victim"/>
<symbol space="ram" offset="0x100a" name="next_func"/>
<symbol space="ram" offset="0x1040" name="__stack_chk_fail"/>
<symbol space="ram" offset="0x1050" name="neighbor_marker"/>
</binaryimage>
<script>
  <com>option funcboundflow off</com>
  <com>option noreturn_externmatch off</com>
  <com>option noreturn_extern off</com>
  <com>lo fu victim</com>
  <com>decompile</com>
  <com>print C</com>
  <com>option noreturn_extern on</com>
  <com>lo fu victim</com>
  <com>decompile</com>
  <com>print C</com>
  <com>quit</com>
  <!-- pass 1 = default (option off) reproduces the bug; pass 2 (option on) is the fix -->
  <!-- (kuna) option noreturn_extern is default-OFF opt-in: its name match overlaps
       noreturn_known's for defined/imported symbols, so default-on would change PE/ELF
       exit-family handling under `noreturn_known off` (verify_multiformat_passes). -->
  <!-- noreturn_externmatch (DIV-13, default-ON) is the always-on companion that
       name-matches the SAME __stack_chk_fail at the SAME flow seam; it is disabled
       up-front (sticks across both passes) to isolate the noreturn_extern toggle this
       test exercises, otherwise pass 1 would already be bounded and `neighbor_marker(`
       would not leak. funcboundflow (DIV-67, default-ON) is a third overlapping
       companion: it bounds the SAME fall-through at neighbor_marker's function entry
       regardless of the callee name, so it too is disabled up-front to keep the leak
       visible in pass 1. -->

</script>
<stringmatch name="noreturn_extern #1: neighbour call leaks past the no-return call (pass 1 off only)" min="1" max="1">neighbor_marker\(</stringmatch>
<stringmatch name="noreturn_extern #3: __stack_chk_fail is the bounding call (both passes)" min="2" max="2">__stack_chk_fail\(</stringmatch>
</decompilertest>
